==Old English==

===Etymology===
(Closely related to [[leode|lēode]] and [[leodan|lēodan]].) From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*liudiz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*lewdʰ-||grow, come up}}. Cognates include Old High German [[liut]], Old Norse ''ljōðr''; and, outside the Germanic languages, Lithuanian [[liaudis|liáudis]] ‘common people’, Old Church Slavonic ''l’udъ'' (Russian [[люд]]).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/leːod/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|m|head=lēod}}

# [[man]], [[chief]], [[leader]]
# {{context|poetic|lang=ang}} a [[prince]]
# a fine for slaying a man, [[wergild]]

